A parent must see only the child or children linked to that parent's demo account.

Use the existing shared school data model wherever possible.

Represent relationships using stable identifiers:

- Parent ID.
- Student ID.
- Parent–student relationship.
- Class and section IDs.
- Academic year and term IDs.
- Attendance records.
- Timetable entries.
- Assignment records.
- Examination and result records.
- Fee records and payment history.
- Announcement records.
- Message or enquiry records, if implemented.

Never associate records merely by matching display names.

If a parent has multiple linked children, provide a child selector that switches the context of the portal.

When switching children, update every relevant section consistently, including attendance, timetable, assignments, results, fee information, and child-specific notices.

Never show one child's records under another child's profile.

If the current demo does not contain parent–student relationships, create a small, coherent set of linked demo records without duplicating existing students.

Provide realistic demonstration data with varied dates and statuses. Derive displayed totals and summaries from those records.

Do not fabricate live information or claim that demo data came from a real school.

If there are no linked children, show a helpful empty state explaining that no student is linked to the parent demo account. Do not silently expose every student in the database.

The frontend's data scoping is for demonstration only. Production deployments must enforce these restrictions on the server.

